# Docker build context exclusions.
#
# The build context is the workspace root, and that is not a choice: hub/hub/ripper_host.py
# derives the vendor root as `parents[2] / "AppleMusicDecrypt"`, so with the package at
# /app/hub/hub/ the client has to land at /app/AppleMusicDecrypt for the derivation to hold.
# Moving either tree breaks the seam in a way that only shows up as a missing config.
#
# The two upstream trees are therefore **excluded, not shipped**: the Dockerfile clones both
# at build time, pinned by commit hash, so a local checkout of either has no place in the
# context -- and `AppleMusicDecrypt/downloads/` alone is tens of GB, with every host venv and
# the host's real config.toml alongside it.
#
# One lesson from when these trees were submodules is still load-bearing, and it is not
# obvious: **git's ignore rules do not apply to a Docker build context.** `wrapper/.gitignore`
# keeps its build/ and its NDK out of *git*, and `AppleMusicDecrypt/.gitignore` keeps
# downloads/ out of git, but Docker reads only this file. So every exclusion below stands on
# its own account, and a rule moved into one of those `.gitignore` files would silently stop
# excluding anything here.

# --- Upstream trees: cloned at build time, not shipped --------------------
# The Dockerfile clones both upstream repos at build time (pinned by commit hash),
# so the local copies must never enter the build context. This replaces the old
# submodule arrangement where the trees were COPYed directly.
AppleMusicDecrypt/
wrapper/

# --- hub: not runtime ---------------------------------------------------
# A host venv would land in the image and its pyvenv.cfg points at the host interpreter.
hub/.venv/
# The runtime image has no pytest and no wrapper path; the test suite and the probe run on
# the host. 1.7 MB, and shipping them would imply they are runnable in there.
hub/tests/
hub/spike/

# --- VCS / docs / cache -------------------------------------------------
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
docs/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/

# --- Secrets and local data --------------------------------------------
# `**/` on every one of these, and that is not decoration. A Docker pattern without a leading
# `**/` is anchored at the context root, so the bare `*.db` that was here matched the context
# root and nothing else: `hub/hub/web/worker-check.db` -- a gitignored test artifact -- was in
# the image for the whole of task 10 and nothing caught it, because the only test that reads
# this file asked about root-anchored paths and therefore agreed with itself.
#
# It matters for what could come next, not just for the 12 KB of empty sqlite that was in
# there: `AMD_DB_PATH=/data/hub.db` is on a volume, but a hub.db created *in the tree* by any
# mundane slip is queue rows -- requested URLs, adam_ids, `skip_reason` strings. That is the
# user's listening history. A pattern that only guards the context root is not a guard.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
**/data/
**/tmp/
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
# The Apple account database, named explicitly as well as by the `**/data/` above, because it
# is the one exclusion whose value cannot be read off a pattern: `wrapper/rootfs/data/` holds a
# real `accounts.sqlitedb`, `cookies.sqlitedb` and `token_cache.json` from a wrapper somebody
# logged in by hand, and `docker history` reads an image layer.
wrapper/rootfs/data/

# --- Editors ------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
